---
/**
 * The Collect landing — the WHOLE of it, once, for both languages.
 *
 * ── WHAT CHANGED, AND WHY IT IS THE POINT ─────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * This family used to own only the closing section. The page's opening — the
 * video, the exoplanet pitch, the Orbiter link, the five-step carousel — was
 * raw HTML inside the two records' bodies, which means it was written TWICE:
 * once in `pages/en/collect/index.md` and once in `pages/es/collect/index.md`.
 * ~45 structural elements each, and by the time MW-19 measured it the two had
 * already drifted apart by two elements. Nobody edited them to diverge. They
 * simply did, because there were two copies and no check could see the
 * difference — the largest drift on the site, and the one the owner spotted
 * unaided by noticing that the Spanish page rendered differently.
 *
 * So the structure is here and the words are on the records. A design change is
 * one edit in this file. A translation is words. That is the whole of MW-19.
 *
 * ── THE THREE PLACES A STRING CAN LIVE, AND WHICH IS WHICH ────────────────────
 *
 *   record `collect`     the page's OWN copy — pitch, captions, its headings.
 *                        See the field in src/content/schemas.mjs.
 *   COLLECT_LANDING      what this FAMILY says whatever page it draws — the
 *                        contact form, the closing pair, the facade's wording.
 *                        Keyed by language, both halves adjacent, exactly like
 *                        LAB_INTRO and for the reason stated there.
 *   nowhere              a URL or an image path. Those have no language: they
 *                        are COLLECT_STORE_URL, COLLECT_VIDEO_URL,
 *                        COLLECT_JOURNEY_IMAGES, COLLECT_BAND_IMAGE.
 *
 * ── THE SECTION COLLAGE IS THIS FAMILY'S JOB, and forgetting that removed it ──
 *
 * `/collect` is one of the six surfaces with a picture chosen for it in
 * SECTION_COLLAGE — `24-archive-explosion` — and that hero was rendered by the
 * route's default branch. Declaring `family: "collect"` took the page out of
 * that branch, so the header silently went with it. A family that renders a
 * page header has to render the whole page header.
 *
 * ── ONE ROW, ONE COMPONENT, TWICE ─────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The closing pair first shipped as a full-width `article` card above a single
 * `entry` card, and the two read as unrelated things that happened to be
 * adjacent — one banner, one object. They are a pair: learn how, then get the
 * deck. So they are the same variant at the same size, side by side, which is
 * the home page's own grid and the answer to "they complement each other".
 *
 * NO SEPARATE ACTION BUTTONS on those two. Each card is one link target, so the
 * words that used to sit on a button beneath it — "watch tutorials", "collect"
 * — are the card's meta line instead. That is exactly what the home page's
 * entry cards do with "visit" and "read".
 *
 * ── WHY THE DEAD THEME'S CLASS NAMES ARE NOT EMITTED HERE ─────────────────────
 *
 * The body markup this replaces was built from `.grid`, `.cell--bordered`,
 * `.hero--dark`, `.hero-example--linear-gradient` and `.p-4`. Those are the
 * dead theme's names, and `styles/legacy.css` says of itself that it "holds only
 * rules keyed to the dead theme's class names and is deleted whole when the
 * migration stops emitting them". A component written today that emitted them
 * would make that sentence permanently false.
 *
 * Nothing is lost by dropping them, and this was checked rather than assumed —
 * read the rules in legacy.css. `.grid` is a flex column with a gap, `.cell` is
 * `width: 100%`, `.hero--dark` explicitly draws `background: none`, and
 * `.hero-example--linear-gradient`, `.p-4` and `.padding: ($spacer * 6);` (a
 * class name that is a CSS declaration, from a Jekyll template that leaked)
 * match no rule in any stylesheet at all. The layout below is those computed
 * results under honest names. It also HAD to be rewritten regardless: every one
 * of those rules is scoped under `.prose`, and this markup no longer sits in
 * the prose slot.
 *
 * The two `.swiper__button` divs per carousel are gone for the same reason —
 * they are Font Awesome chevrons for a script that no longer exists, and
 * `ui/CarouselScript` builds its own arrows at runtime.
 *
 * ── AND WHERE THE TWO HALVES DISAGREED, ENGLISH WON ───────────────────────────
 *
 * The owner's decision of 2026-08-01, and it had to be applied twice here:
 *
 *   the two buttons   English drew them as `button--primary button--rounded
 *                     button--xl` with a bare word inside; Spanish drew them as
 *                     `button--buy` / `button` with an inline SVG icon. There is
 *                     no single legacy spelling to preserve when the two halves
 *                     disagree, so they are `ui/Action` — the site's own
 *                     component, whose whole purpose is that a page family does
 *                     not spell button classes for itself. The Spanish icons
 *                     came off, on the condition that if they came back they
 *                     came back once, for both languages.
 *
 *                     THEY CAME BACK, and on that condition: `ui/Action` takes
 *                     an `icon`, and it is set below on the call rather than in
 *                     either record, so `en` and `es` cannot drift apart again.
 *                     They are not the Spanish half's SVGs restored — they are
 *                     the site's own symbols for these destinations, `category`
 *                     for collect and `satellite_alt` for an Orbiter, the same
 *                     ones the header uses.
 *   the opening line  the Spanish body repeated the record's own `description`
 *                     as a paragraph under the H1. Both records pass that
 *                     sentence to the collage as `statement`, so `/es/collect`
 *                     was printing its opening sentence twice. The English half
 *                     does not, so it does not.
 */
import Card from '../patterns/Card.astro';
import CollageField from '../patterns/CollageField.astro';
import Action from '../ui/Action.astro';
import Statement from '../patterns/Statement.astro';
import Carousel from '../media/Carousel.astro';
import EmbedFacade from '../media/EmbedFacade.astro';
import {
  COLLECT_BAND_IMAGE,
  COLLECT_COVERS,
  COLLECT_JOURNEY_IMAGES,
  COLLECT_LANDING,
  COLLECT_STORE_URL,
  COLLECT_VIDEO_URL,
  collageFor,
} from '../../config/site';
import { facadeStrings } from '../../config/embeds';

interface Slide {
  step: string;
  caption: string;
}

interface Props {
  /** Seed for the cards' type marks — the page's own outputPath. */
  seed: string;
  /** The page's own label, for the visual header's accessible name. */
  label: string;
  /** The page's language. Decides every word on the page that is not a prop. */
  lang: string;
  /** The `<h1>`'s inner HTML. It already carries its type mark. */
  headingHtml?: string;
  /** Fallback when a record carries no marked heading — the plain page title. */
  title: string;
  /**
   * The page's opening sentence, which rides the collage as a moving field of
   * cut words rather than sitting under it as a paragraph.
   *
   * Same component and same movement as the home page — `patterns/PaperPhrase`
   * through `CollageField`'s `statement` slot. It is read from the record's
   * `description` and is NOT also in the body: see the note above about the
   * Spanish half, which used to print it a second time.
   */
  statement?: string;
  /** The page's own copy. The record's `collect` field, verbatim. */
  copy: {
    pitch: { title: string; body: string; cta: string };
    orbiters: { label: string };
    journey: { title: string; carouselLabel: string; slides: Slide[] };
  };
  /**
   * Where the Orbiters link goes, in this page's language.
   *
   * Resolved by the route from the records themselves rather than written here,
   * because that is the one rule `verify:translations` already asserts for the
   * header — "a Spanish page's navigation stays in Spanish". A page that sent a
   * Spanish reader to `/orbiters` would lose them the language in one click.
   */
  orbitersHref: string;
  /**
   * The id every carousel slide is anchored on. The route derives it from the
   * record's own outputPath, so the two halves of a pair cannot collide.
   */
  carouselId: string;
  /** Whether the record's body holds anything a reader would see. */
  hasBody?: boolean;
}

const {
  seed,
  label,
  lang,
  headingHtml,
  title,
  statement,
  copy,
  orbitersHref,
  carouselId,
  hasBody = false,
} = Astro.props;

const strings = COLLECT_LANDING[lang] ?? COLLECT_LANDING.en;
const { method, offer, form, pairLabel } = strings;

/**
 * What the facade says, from the site-wide table rather than from this family.
 *
 * It used to be `COLLECT_LANDING.video`, with the note beside it saying that the
 * pair would want "one shared table keyed by provider and language" once the
 * other conversions arrived. They have — `src/config/embeds.ts` — so this reads
 * from it, and the sentence exists once for every facade on the site instead of
 * once per family and once per page body.
 *
 * The fallback matches the line above it: an unknown language is drawn in
 * English rather than failing, because a landing with no words is worse than a
 * landing in the wrong ones.
 */
const video = facadeStrings('youtube', lang === 'es' ? 'es' : 'en');
const collage = collageFor(seed);

/**
 * The photographs and their captions, zipped — and a BUILD FAILURE if the two
 * lists are not the same length.
 *
 * The pictures have no language and live in config; the captions do and live on
 * the record. That split is right and it has exactly one failure mode: the two
 * lists coming to disagree about how many slides there are. Left unchecked that
 * publishes a slide with no words under it, or drops a photograph, silently and
 * on one language only — which is the precise shape of failure this whole issue
 * exists to stop, reintroduced one level down.
 *
 * So it throws, with both counts and the file to open. A record without a
 * required field already fails the build (see `lang` in schemas.mjs); this is
 * the same standard applied to a relation a schema cannot see, because the
 * schema cannot count something that lives in a TypeScript module.
 */
if (copy.journey.slides.length !== COLLECT_JOURNEY_IMAGES.length) {
  throw new Error(
    `[collect] ${seed}: ${copy.journey.slides.length} journey captions for ` +
      `${COLLECT_JOURNEY_IMAGES.length} photographs. They are zipped by position — ` +
      'add or remove a caption in the record\'s `collect.journey.slides`, or a path in ' +
      'COLLECT_JOURNEY_IMAGES in src/config/site.ts, so the two agree.',
  );
}

const slides = COLLECT_JOURNEY_IMAGES.map((image, i) => ({
  image,
  step: copy.journey.slides[i].step,
  caption: copy.journey.slides[i].caption,
}));
---

<div class="collect">
  {
    /* The same compact visual introduction the other five section pages use.
       It carries the page's opening sentence as a moving field of cut words, so
       it is the page's whole header: the H1 below is kept for document
       structure and taken out of sight. */
    collage && (
      <CollageField
        src={collage}
        variant="section"
        label={`${label} visual header`}
        statement={statement}
        statementSeed={`${seed}:statement`}
      />
    )
  }

  {/* Hidden, never removed — see the note on `.collect__title` in the style
      block below. It was the body's first line until MW-19; it is a field now,
      the same way family 01's is, because a heading carrying a type mark is
      markup and markup is what this page stopped keeping two copies of. */}
  <h1 class="collect__title">
    {headingHtml ? <Fragment set:html={headingHtml} /> : title}
  </h1>

  {/**
   * EVERY BAND CARRIES `.prose`, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING RATHER THAN TIDY.
   *
   * The facade's ENTIRE stylesheet is written as `.prose .embed-facade…` — 20
   * rules in styles/legacy.css — because until now a facade only ever existed
   * inside a record body, and a record body is rendered inside `.prose`. Markup
   * moved out of the slot leaves that scope, so a facade outside it renders as
   * an unstyled paragraph with a link in it. The same is true of the carousel's
   * interaction with `prose.css`'s image containment, and of the type rhythm on
   * these headings and paragraphs.
   *
   * So the scope comes along. It is exactly what the contact form below already
   * does, and for the same stated reason: the markup moved position, not shape.
   */}
  {/* The video, click-to-load. Nothing is requested from YouTube until the
      reader chooses it — the invariant in AGENTS.md, and the reason this is a
      facade rather than an iframe. */}
  <section class="prose collect__band collect__band--center" aria-label={copy.pitch.title}>
    <EmbedFacade
      provider="youtube"
      href={COLLECT_VIDEO_URL}
      label={video.label}
      note={video.note}
    />

    {/* The page's opening statement, in the Lab h1's voice. See
        `patterns/statement` for why an h2 is allowed a mark here and what that
        exception does not extend to. */}
    <Statement text={copy.pitch.title} mark={copy.pitch.mark} />
    <p class="collect__lede">{copy.pitch.body}</p>
    {/* `category` is collect's own symbol in `AREAS` — the same one the header
        wears on this area and the home page wears on "collect cards". The icon
        is named here rather than in the record because it belongs to the
        destination, not to the sentence: `en` says "Collect" and `es` says
        "Coleccionar", and both are the same action. */}
    {/* Collect's one stamp. The store is what this page is for, and v4 allows
        the lift on exactly the primary action of a view. The tilt sign comes
        from the pigment rather than from here — "maar tilts left, collect tilts
        right" — so this leans the opposite way to the home page's stamp without
        either call site choosing an angle. */}
    <Action
      href={COLLECT_STORE_URL}
      label={copy.pitch.cta}
      variant="collect"
      emphasis="stamp"
      icon="category"
    />
  </section>

  {/* A picture and nothing else. In the migrated body this was a background
      image on a hero whose only content was an `<h2>` holding four `<br>`s —
      a spacer wearing a heading's clothes, and an empty heading in the page
      outline. It is an image with an empty alt because it is decoration. */}
  <img class="collect__banner" src={COLLECT_BAND_IMAGE} alt="" />

  <section class="prose collect__band collect__band--center" aria-label={copy.orbiters.label}>
    {/* The link asks what an Orbiter is, so it wears what an Orbiter is: the
        `satellite_alt` the header already gives `/orbiters` in `SECTIONS`. */}
    {/* Collect's one break, and the page's whole movement budget is now spent.
        Tape rather than pivot: this link asks what an Orbiter is, which is a
        question rather than a direction, and v4 reserves the pivot for "anything
        with a direction". A strip of tape across the corner is the aside it
        actually is. It is legal here because the button is unfilled — tape on a
        pigment field would be two pastels touching, which `ui/action` refuses. */}
    <Action
      href={orbitersHref}
      label={copy.orbiters.label}
      variant="secondary"
      gesture="tape"
      icon="satellite_alt"
    />
  </section>

  <section class="prose collect__band" aria-labelledby="collect-journey">
    {/* Keeps its id: the section is labelled by this heading, so the element
        the `aria-labelledby` points at must stay put. */}
    <Statement
      text={copy.journey.title}
      mark={copy.journey.mark}
      id="collect-journey"
      align="center"
    />
    <Carousel id={carouselId} label={copy.journey.carouselLabel} slides={slides} />
  </section>

  {/* Learn how, then get the deck. One grid, one variant, two cards. */}
  <section class="collect__pair" aria-label={pairLabel}>
    <Card
      variant="entry"
      href={method.href}
      title={method.title}
      level={2}
      cover={COLLECT_COVERS.method}
      excerpt={method.excerpt}
      meta={method.meta}
      seed={`${seed}:method`}
    />
    <Card
      variant="entry"
      href={COLLECT_STORE_URL}
      title={offer.title}
      level={2}
      cover={COLLECT_COVERS.offer}
      excerpt={offer.excerpt}
      meta={offer.meta}
      seed={`${seed}:offer`}
    />
  </section>

  {
    /* Whatever prose the record still holds. Both halves hold none today — the
       page is entirely structure and fields — but the slot stays: a landing
       that can never carry a paragraph is a landing nobody can add one to.

       Rendered only when there IS something, because both bodies now carry a
       note explaining where their markup went, and markdown passes an HTML
       comment through to the page. See `bodyHasContent` in [...page].astro. */
    hasBody && (
      <div class="prose collect__body">
        <slot />
      </div>
    )
  }

  {/* The contact form, in a `.prose` wrapper so every rule styles/button.css
      already writes for `.prose .form-container`, its grid and its fields
      applies exactly as before. It moved position, not shape.

      THE ENDPOINT IS WRITTEN ONCE. It used to be inside this component already;
      what is new is that its LABELS come from COLLECT_LANDING, so the Spanish
      page stops asking for "Your name" under Spanish body copy. */}
  <div class="prose">
    <div class="form-container">
      <h2>{form.heading}</h2>
      <p>{form.lede}</p>

      <form action="https://formspree.io/f/xjvneyen" method="POST" class="contact-form">
        <label>
          {form.name}
          <input type="text" name="name" required />
        </label>
        <label>
          {form.email}
          <input type="email" name="email" required />
        </label>
        <label>
          {form.message}
          <textarea name="message" required></textarea>
        </label>
        <button type="submit">{form.submit}</button>
      </form>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  /**
   * THE PAGE HAS AN H1 AND DOES NOT SHOW IT.
   *
   * The owner's call, 2026-07-31: once the collage carries the wordmark and the
   * opening sentence rides it as a marquee, a second "collect" underneath is the
   * page naming itself twice.
   *
   * It is HIDDEN, NEVER REMOVED. `verify:a11y` asserts every page has exactly
   * one `<h1>`, and that check is right — a document with no first heading is
   * unnavigable by heading, and the assertion would have caught deleting it.
   * The same reasoning and the same treatment as `.home__title--accessible` on
   * family 01, which hides its H1 because the brand link already says the word.
   *
   * `clip` rather than `display: none` for exactly that reason: the heading
   * stays in the accessibility tree and in the text `verify:content` measures.
   *
   * It used to be written as `.collect__body > h1:first-child`, because the
   * heading arrived through the slot as the body's first line. The heading is a
   * field now, so the rule names the element itself — and stops depending on a
   * record happening to open with one.
   */
  .collect__title {
    position: absolute;
    inline-size: 1px;
    block-size: 1px;
    padding: 0;
    margin: -1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip: rect(0 0 0 0);
    white-space: nowrap;
    border: 0;
  }

  /* The same rhythm the home family uses — "sibling groups use gap, never
     margins" — so the two landings read as one site rather than two. */
  .collect {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--s-16);
    padding-block: 0 var(--s-24);
  }

  /**
   * A band of the page.
   *
   * This is `.prose .grid` + `.cell` + `.hero` from styles/legacy.css, computed
   * out: a flex column with the theme's own gap, full width, and the block
   * padding that gave each band its rhythm. `.hero--dark` contributed
   * `background: none` on this surface, so it contributes nothing here either.
   */
  .collect__band {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--s-4);
    padding-block: var(--s-8);
    inline-size: 100%;
  }

  /* `.hero--center` — the theme centred both the text and the items in it. */
  .collect__band--center {
    text-align: center;
    align-items: center;
  }

  /**
   * PROSE OWNS THE TYPE RHYTHM IN A BAND, AND IS LEFT TO.
   *
   * Every band carries `.prose`, so `prose.css` sets the heading and paragraph
   * margins — which is exactly what these elements had inside the migrated
   * body, alongside the theme's flex gap. Reproducing the gap and then zeroing
   * the margins would be a change of spacing dressed as a refactor, so it is
   * not done. Note also that a rule here could not win it anyway: Astro scopes
   * these selectors with `:where()`, which adds no specificity, so
   * `.collect__heading` (0,1,0) loses to `.prose h2` (0,1,1).
   */
  .collect__heading--center {
    text-align: center;
  }

  /* The measure still applies to a paragraph — a centred line 1180px wide is
     not readable, and the band is the full column. */
  .collect__lede {
    max-inline-size: var(--measure);
  }

  /**
   * The decorative band.
   *
   * The theme drew this as a background image on an empty hero, which meant its
   * height came from four `<br>` tags and its aspect ratio came from nothing.
   * As an `<img>` it keeps its own shape, scales with the column, and is one
   * element rather than a heading with no text in it.
   */
  .collect__banner {
    display: block;
    inline-size: 100%;
    block-size: auto;
    border-radius: var(--r-0);
  }

  /**
   * Two cards, side by side from 900px.
   *
   * The pair is deliberately NOT the home grid's three-at-1280 step: there are
   * two of these and there will stay two, so a third column would leave a gap
   * where a card is not. Below 900px they stack, which is the same width every
   * other grid on this site stacks at.
   */
  .collect__pair {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s-6);
  }

  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .collect__pair {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }

</style>
